From d64521eb960cfa421c3d20105c04b6a45f71274d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dist <build@localhost>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 20:41:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] win32u: cache same-thread hook chains, invalidated by a
 shared-memory serial
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Idle Live 12 keeps ~5,700 wineserver requests/sec alive on an empty
set; ~2,424/sec of them are WH_GETMESSAGE hook bookkeeping. Live
installs a thread-local same-process WH_GETMESSAGE hook, so every
retrieved posted/hardware message runs call_hooks(WH_GETMESSAGE,...)
(posted: dlls/win32u/message.c:3245, hardware: message.c:3176) ->
call_message_hooks, which pays three server round trips per message:
start_hook_chain, get_hook_info via NtUserCallNextHookEx, and
finish_hook_chain. The zero-RT gate already existed: is_hooked()
reads queue_shm->hooks_count[id] from shared memory via a seqlock
(get_shared_queue, dlls/win32u/winstation.c).

This patch turns the whole chain walk into a client-side snapshot.
The server gains a hooks_serial in queue_shm_t (server/protocol.def;
the struct grows from 96 to 100 bytes, far below the ~300-byte
object_shm_t union members, so the union size does not move), bumped
inside add_queue_hook_count (server/queue.c) — the single choke
point reached by every hook mutation (add_hook, remove_hook,
remove_thread_hooks and add_desktop_hook_count, server/hook.c).
Client session shared memory views are MAP_SHARED read-write but
server-written only (patches 0018/0019 rule), which this honors:
only the server writes the serial. The regenerated
include/wine/server_protocol.h rides along (tools/make_requests;
nothing in the build regenerates it, and upstream commits the
generated file together with protocol.def); the struct change bumps
SERVER_PROTOCOL_VERSION.

In win32u each thread keeps a per-hook-id snapshot — serial plus an
ordered entry list (handle, proc, unicode flag, module string) — in
its user_thread_info, lazily allocated and freed from thread_detach.
call_message_hooks reads hooks_count and hooks_serial in one seqlock
section: a zero count still skips the chain with no round trip, and
a snapshot whose serial matches is walked by calling the first entry
through the existing call_hook, so the thread_info->hook /
hook_unicode bookkeeping (dlls/win32u/hook.c) is unchanged — zero
round trips per message. NtUserCallNextHookEx finds the current hook
in the snapshots valid at the current serial and calls the next
entry the same way; when the hook is in no current snapshot (serial
moved mid-chain, chain never cached) it falls back to the server
get_hook_info call, which resolves the live chain exactly as today.
A serial miss refills by performing exactly today's sequence —
start_hook_chain + get_hook_info(get_next=1) until exhausted +
finish_hook_chain, recording each reply — so a refill costs no extra
requests; the snapshot is published only when the serial is
unchanged across the walk, so a concurrent mutation can never
validate an inconsistent chain.

v1 restrictions, all falling back to today's full server path: only
replies with pid == tid == 0 (run-in-current-thread) are recorded, so
low-level hooks rerouted to their owner thread
(run_hook_in_owner_thread, server/hook.c) are not cached, and a
chain containing one is marked not cacheable at that serial.
WH_WINEVENT chains are excluded entirely: their server-side walk
posts win events as a side effect (get_first_valid_hook /
get_next_hook, server/hook.c).

Semantics preserved, point by point:

- Walk order matches the server's: the snapshot records the replies
  in the order start_hook_chain/get_hook_info return them (queue
  chain most-recent-first, then the global/desktop chain).
- The server's chain-in-use refcount (server/hook.c) is not taken on
  the cached path: the snapshot only holds values, and any removal
  bumps the serial before the next message. Residual delta, only
  observable with >= 2 hooks in one chain: a hook that unhooks
  itself mid-chain and then calls CallNextHookEx gets the
  server-fallback error path (its handle is already freed, since the
  cached walk never took the chain refcount) and the rest of the
  chain is skipped, where the refcounted server walk would still
  reach the later hooks.
- The module string is cached per entry and passed through
  unchanged, so the GetModuleHandleExW semantics of the user32
  callback side (dlls/user32/hook.c) are identical.
- The refill pairs start/finish_hook_chain exactly like today, so
  the server's chain-in-use counts balance on refills and on every
  fallback path.
- WINE_MSG_FASTPATH (on by default; "0"/"off" disables,
  case-insensitive) gates this and the following two patches; with
  it off every path is byte-identical to today.

Verification: full series (62 main + performance 0001/0002 + this)
applies with git am --3way onto the pristine base; server/queue.o,
dlls/win32u/{hook,sysparams}.o compile warning-free with Wine's
warning set; tools/make_requests regenerates server_protocol.h with
hooks_serial.
